    {
      "id": "decl-meaning",
      "type": "page-section",
      "label": "Meaning",
      "position": {
        "x": -180,
        "y": -640
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 320,
      "data": {
        "title": "Meaning",
        "body": "**What is this thing?**\n\nThe root is the node a graph begins from. It carries the graph's durable identity, intent, authority and dependencies, and the Surfaces it shows. It is the graph's semantic anchor and its implicit root Port: the entry point every consumer reaches first, without inventing a separate node merely for plumbing.\n\n*Root* is a verb as well. To root graphs is to select several of them in a workspace and make a new graph from them. (Rooting is not built yet.)\n\nA graph without a root is a fragment: content that hasn't yet been given an identity of its own.\n\nA graph is a scene by default. Its nodes are placed by hand and its edges are drawn, and that arrangement is what a reader sees. When the root's `ground` edge reaches a pinned node, that node is the ground beneath the graph: a picture, a poster or a page.",
        "presentation": {
          "glyph": {
            "kind": "icon",
            "name": "Article"
          },
          "baseLevel": "detail"
        },
        "_placement": {
          "version": 1,
          "state": "auto-placed",
          "source": "bridge",
          "updatedAt": 1791592052360,
          "reason": "bridge-reference"
        },
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "ac97492b-7402-428b-9bce-5c5e91c7f981"
        },
        "classRef": "tlz://twilite.zone/library/templates/page-template/classes/page-section.node-class.node"
      },
      "inputs": [
        {
          "key": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "type": "any",
          "position": {
            "side": "top",
            "offset": 0.2
          }
        }
      ],
      "outputs": [
        {
          "key": "root",
          "label": "root",
